# rover_can

Generic SocketCAN hardware interface for the rover comms library. One node per
CAN loop. It moves raw frames between the bus and ROS2 topics and does not
encode or decode payloads — that stays in the comms library, on whichever node
owns the topic.

## Topics

| Topic | Direction | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `/can/<loop_name>/incoming` | node → you | every frame that has no typed topic |
| `/can/<loop_name>/<message_type>` | node → you | optional per-type topics |
| `/can/<loop_name>/outgoing` | you → node | anything published here is written to the bus |

All topics use `rover_msgs/msg/GenericCommsMsg`.

## Layout

```
rover_comms/CMakeLists.txt           ament wrapper around the comms C library
rover_msgs/msg/GenericCommsMsg.msg   the message
rover_can/src/socket_can.{h,cpp}     raw socket, no ROS2 in it
rover_can/src/can_topic_routing.h    the switch case: type -> topic name
rover_can/src/can_node.{h,cpp}       the node
rover_can/config/can_loop.yaml       node params + link settings
rover_can/launch/can_loop.launch.py  one loop
rover_can/scripts/can_setup.sh       ip link bring up
```

## Build

`rover_comms` is a thin ament wrapper that builds the shared comms C library and
exports `comms.h` on the include path. `rover_can` just depends on it, so id
packing and CAN frame conversion go through the library rather than being
reimplemented in the node.

Point `rover_comms` at the comms sources — default is a submodule or symlink at
`rover_comms/comms`:

```bash
colcon build --packages-select rover_comms rover_msgs rover_can \
  --cmake-args -DCOMMS_DIR=/abs/path/to/comms
```

This requires `comms_id_helpers.patch` to be applied first — `comms.c` does not
currently compile, and `comms.h` does not compile as C.

## Run

Bring the interface up once after plugging in the CANable:

```bash
./can_setup.sh --interface can0 --bitrate 500000 --sample-point 0.875 --restart-ms 100
```

Then:

```bash
ros2 launch rover_can can_loop.launch.py loop_name:=main can_interface:=can0
```

Or let the launch file do the bring-up (needs passwordless sudo for `ip`):

```bash
ros2 launch rover_can can_loop.launch.py loop_name:=main bring_up_interface:=true
```

Multiple loops: include `can_loop.launch.py` once per loop with a different
`loop_name`, `can_interface`, and optionally `config_file`.

## Adding a typed topic

Add a case to `type_to_topic_suffix()` in `can_topic_routing.h`. That is the
only edit — the node walks every message type at startup and creates a
publisher for anything that returns a name.

## Gotchas

- The interface is a udev-level thing. If the CANable is unplugged the node
  dies; the launch file sets `respawn=True` so it comes back once the interface
  returns (though you will need to re-run `can_setup.sh`, since a re-enumerated
  interface comes back down).
- `ENOBUFS` on write means the tx queue filled up. Usually that means nothing
  else on the bus is ACKing frames — check termination and that another node is
  actually powered.
- The node only accepts 29-bit extended ids, because that is what comms uses.
  Standard-id traffic is logged (throttled) and dropped.
- If a message type you expected on its own topic keeps landing in `incoming`,
  look for the throttled "no typed topic" warning. It prints the type id that
  actually came off the bus, which is almost always a `msg_type_t` mismatch
  between the sender's firmware and this build.
- CAN FD frames longer than `MAX_MESSAGE_PAYLOAD_SIZE_BYTES` are dropped, since
  comms' `can_msg_t` cannot hold them.
- `sender_id` on the outgoing topic is ignored. The node stamps its own
  `device_id` parameter on everything it transmits. If `device_id` is left at
  the invalid default the node still receives, but refuses to send.